# Line-ending policy, declared rather than left to each contributor's core.autocrlf.
#
# The scripts here are byte-sensitive in one way that a surprise re-encoding can break: launch-rig.ps1 contains
# Chinese (a world folder name) and must stay UTF-8 WITH a BOM, because the powershell 5.1 that runs it would
# otherwise decode the file as ANSI and mangle the path. Several other scripts carry a BOM for the same reason.
#
# .gitattributes does not touch the BOM - only line endings - but pinning the endings removes one variable from a
# failure mode that is genuinely hard to attribute after the fact.
#
# eol=lf, not eol=crlf: the scripts are LF today and PowerShell runs them fine either way, so declaring what is
# already true avoids a one-off rewrite of every script the next time git touches the tree. It also wins over the
# machine's core.autocrlf=true, so a clone gets the same bytes everywhere.
*.ps1  text eol=lf

*.md   text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
